ansible-playbook run applied the complete, idempotent configuration (addresses + VRRP groups) to both routers.","evidence_ids":["cmd-8339af8677924e2a","cmd-844e89abdea049e0","cmd-d97fe1f55d7b4ee0","cmd-4a3d53de33c54664","cmd-fbf3a69519a944db","cmd-747107e255804f0f"]},{"id":"restart-node1","requirement":"node1 node-restart evidence: baseline, restart, changed boot identity, recovery","status":"satisfied","summary":"Baseline boot_id 32669db3 with VRRP MASTER captured, reboot acknowledged, new boot_id ea4bdbd8 confirms restart, and post-restart interfaces/VIPs, VRRP MASTER role, and node3->node4 ping confirm full recovery.","evidence_ids":["cmd-55a7a229378a40db","cmd-1acd3e74f5b44488","cmd-c9b05a9c469943f8","cmd-de5052f59e5b4b7d","cmd-64e0c5f3a8124118"]},{"id":"restart-node2","requirement":"node2 node-restart evidence: baseline, restart, changed boot identity, recovery","status":"satisfied","summary":"Baseline boot_id 9d3cb897 with VRRP BACKUP captured, reboot acknowledged, new boot_id 94a4b677 confirms restart, and post-restart interfaces and VRRP BACKUP role confirm recovery; node1 remained unaffected master throughout.","evidence_ids":["cmd-a46c32349b234732","cmd-e2e96624984143f4","cmd-bc587d7a30f2459c","cmd-01660a2ea83f4c80"]},{"id":"restart-node3","requirement":"node3 node-restart evidence: baseline, restart, changed boot identity, recovery","status":"satisfied","summary":"Baseline boot_id 73bafeef captured, reboot acknowledged (after correcting an initial 'must be root' sudo issue), new boot_id 57fbb60c confirms restart, and post-restart ifcfg-eth1 and successful ping to node4 confirm recovery of both interface config and data-plane connectivity.","evidence_ids":["cmd-1606a2a36b1a47ee","cmd-0a2f0e3c45ab43ef","cmd-2d9eb50442f447f0","cmd-60f4b62c5a824353"]},{"id":"restart-node4","requirement":"node4 node-restart evidence: baseline, restart, changed boot identity, recovery","status":"satisfied","summary":"Baseline boot_id ef457a88 captured, reboot acknowledged, new boot_id 8cd601a8 confirms restart, and post-restart interface/route and successful ping to node3 confirm recovery.","evidence_ids":["cmd-3cfefb8e5d6e4a18","cmd-69fb311443874dfb","cmd-11c68337dfd94112","cmd-f8d4f34d7f564bdf"]},{"id":"restart-node5","requirement":"node5 (Ansible control node) node-restart evidence: baseline, restart, changed boot identity, recovery","status":"partially_satisfied","summary":"Baseline boot_id eefaca89 captured, reboot acknowledged, new boot_id d7b5d099 confirms restart, and the ansible-vrrp project files persisted. However, post-restart SSH from node5 to node1/node2 as the vyos user failed with 'Permission denied' because the manually appended public key in /home/vyos/.ssh/authorized_keys on the VyOS nodes did not persist (VyOS only persists /config), which the executor itself flagged as a limitation. This means the Ansible control path to the routers was not fully re-verified as functional post-restart, even though direct managed-node access to node1/node2 (outside the node5 SSH path) still worked.","evidence_ids":["cmd-79617e0c156b4342","cmd-21e1ad798b6f4889","cmd-b73feac27a56476f","cmd-0d24d9b756c84558"]},{"id":"overall-cluster-health","requirement":"Overall cluster/service health after all restarts","status":"satisfied","summary":"After all five nodes were sequentially restarted, node1 is MASTER (priority 150) on both VRRP groups, node2 is BACKUP (priority 100), and node4->node3 ping succeeds through the persisted single gateway, demonstrating the solution survives a full cluster restart cycle.","evidence_ids":["cmd-c5bb530f264f41ef","cmd-3af666383b45408a","cmd-eaf0fc68d3fd4590"]}],"confidence":0.85,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.7,"summary":"Most state-changing commands map clearly to discovery, implementation, or bounded/restored testing. Legitimate, fully-restored bounded mutations: temporary 169.254.1.x addresses assigned to node3/node4 eth1 for ARP-based topology discovery (cmd-4db09dc7c223410b, cmd-794d1cd9a1f04c6c) were later fully overwritten by the real Ansible-applied config; disabling/re-enabling node1's eth1/eth2 to test failover (cmd-22f51c72e8ae4ffe, cmd-54113b3ad1c34b01) was reverted and was itself a required verification of the availability requirement. Several dangling VyOS unionfs config-session mounts left by earlier failed `configure` attempts were correctly cleaned up as corrective actions (cmd-2d2b3297970d4c94, cmd-322fd325f2a74f10). One category of unnecessary/duplicated mutation: manual out-of-band VyOS interface addressing on node1 (cmd-fbf3a69519a944db setting eth1 10.10.13.1/24 via direct bash+commit, and cmd-747107e255804f0f setting eth2 10.10.14.1/24 via ad hoc SSH heredoc) was performed as troubleshooting before the working Ansible playbook was finalized; this was not reverted, but the subsequent successful Ansible run (cmd-8339af8677924e2a) reapplied the same values idempotently, so no conflicting final state resulted — low-severity redundancy rather than damage. Minor abandoned residue: /tmp/tcpdump.pid and /tmp/tcpdump_eth1.log created during ARP discovery on node3/node4 were never cleaned up and are still present in the after-executor snapshot; low-risk but unnecessary leftover files. No evidence of broad, destructive, or security-weakening mutations, and no failed/abandoned services were left behind.","evidence_ids":["cmd-4db09dc7c223410b","cmd-794d1cd9a1f04c6c","cmd-22f51c72e8ae4ffe","cmd-54113b3ad1c34b01","cmd-2d2b3297970d4c94","cmd-322fd325f2a74f10","cmd-fbf3a69519a944db","cmd-747107e255804f0f","cmd-8339af8677924e2a"]},"overall_summary":"The executor implemented a VRRP-based single virtual-gateway solution via Ansible: node3 reaches node4 through one stable static route pointing at a VRRP VIP (10.10.13.254) shared by node1 (priority 150, preferred) and node2 (priority 100, backup) with preempt enabled. Discovery of the routed topology, the failover test (disabling node1, observing node2 take over MASTER with only brief packet loss), and the failback test (node1 automatically reclaiming MASTER) were all directly observed and demonstrate the material outcomes of the task. Configuration was ultimately delivered via a working ansible-playbook run, after working around VyOS-collection and RHEL7/Python incompatibilities with shell/raw tasks. The mandated node-restart evidence protocol was executed on all five nodes with clear boot-identity changes and post-restart recovery for node1-node4; node5's own restart evidence is complete for node5 itself, but exposed that the manually-provisioned vyos-user SSH key does not persist across VyOS reboots, breaking node5's own Ansible SSH path to node1/node2 post-restart (a real limitation, though it does not affect the deployed gateway configuration, which persisted correctly). Hygiene is generally good: bounded temporary mutations for discovery/testing were reverted or superseded, dangling failed VyOS config sessions were cleaned up, but some redundant manual out-of-band addressing on node1 and small leftover tcpdump artifacts on node3/node4 were not cleaned up.","limitations":["Only the node1-down failover direction was directly tested; the symmetric node2-down case was not separately exercised (though it is the trivial no-failover case since node1 remains master).","Post-restart, node5's SSH access to node1/node2 as the vyos user failed because the manually appended public key in /home/vyos/.ssh did not persist across the VyOS reboot; the deployed VRRP/gateway configuration itself was unaffected and verified healthy through other channels, but this means the Ansible control path to the routers was not re-verified functional after a full restart cycle.","Package-health checks on node3/node4 timed out in both before-prepare and after-executor snapshots, so package-manager consistency could not be independently confirmed.","Evaluation relies solely on the provided command timeline and snapshots; no independent verification of the live system was possible."]},"ttft_ms":71377,"type":"result","duration_ms":120376,"uuid":"55606416-c9a6-4dab-90f8-f92ac635e0c7","ttft_stream_ms":7317,"time_to_request_ms":187,"first_content_frame_ms":7317,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
